> Okay. I've also heard about problems with first converting from reais to USD, then using the US deflator, vs. first using national deflators than then converting to USD. What are the effects of this?
**assistant**:
> The two procedures answer **different questions**, because exchange rates, Brazilian inflation, and US inflation do not generally move together.
> 
> Let:
> 
> - 𝑋𝑡: nominal amount in reais in year 𝑡
> - 𝐸𝑡: market exchange rate in BRL per USD in year 𝑡
> - 𝑃𝐵⁢𝑅𝑡: Brazilian price index
> - 𝑃𝑈⁢𝑆𝑡: US price index
> - 0: a chosen base year
> 
> ## 1. Convert to current USD, then deflate with the US deflator
> 
> 𝑋𝑈⁢𝑆⁢𝐷0𝐴=𝑋𝑡𝐸𝑡⁢𝑃𝑈⁢𝑆0𝑃𝑈⁢𝑆𝑡.
> 
> This says:
> 
> > “What was this amount worth at the **market exchange rate of year 𝑡**, expressed in the purchasing power of **base-year US dollars**?”
> 
> This is usually sensible for something inherently valued in internationally traded currency or financial terms: e.g., foreign debt, export revenues, traded commodities, international investment, or a model result whose monetary numeraire is explicitly USD at market exchange rates.
> 
> But it embeds exchange-rate variation. A sharp real depreciation of the real makes the value look much smaller in USD, even if its ability to buy Brazilian goods and services was unchanged.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. Deflate in reais, then convert at a base-year exchange rate
> 
> 𝑋𝑈⁢𝑆⁢𝐷0𝐵=𝑋𝑡⁢𝑃𝐵⁢𝑅0𝑃𝐵⁢𝑅𝑡⁢1𝐸0.
> 
> This says:
> 
> > “First, what is the amount in **constant base-year Brazilian reais**? Then what was that worth at the **base-year** BRL/USD exchange rate?”
> 
> This is more appropriate if the underlying quantity is a **domestic Brazilian real resource**—for example, local household consumption, local public expenditure, or domestic damages—where you want to remove Brazilian price inflation before expressing results in a common currency unit.
> 
> It does *not* treat later movements in the market exchange rate as changes in the real domestic quantity.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Why they differ
> 
> Their ratio is
> 
> 𝑋𝑈⁢𝑆⁢𝐷0𝐴𝑋𝑈⁢𝑆⁢𝐷0𝐵=𝐸0𝐸𝑡⁢𝑃𝐵⁢𝑅𝑡/𝑃𝐵⁢𝑅0𝑃𝑈⁢𝑆𝑡/𝑃𝑈⁢𝑆0.
> 
> This is essentially the change in the **real exchange rate** between Brazil and the United States.
> 
> Thus, the discrepancy reflects:
> 
> 1. **nominal depreciation/appreciation** of the real; and  
> 2. **inflation differentials** between Brazil and the US.
> 
> They would give the same answer only if exchange-rate changes exactly offset the relative inflation difference—i.e., a purchasing-power-parity-type relationship holds over the period.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Simple illustration
> 
> Suppose, from the base year to year 𝑡:
> 
> - Brazilian prices doubled: 𝑃𝐵⁢𝑅𝑡/𝑃𝐵⁢𝑅0 =2.0;
> - US prices rose 20%: 𝑃𝑈⁢𝑆𝑡/𝑃𝑈⁢𝑆0 =1.2;
> - exchange rate moved from 𝐸0 =5 BRL/USD to 𝐸𝑡 =7.5 BRL/USD;
> - the nominal amount is 𝑋𝑡 =100 BRL.
> 
> ### Convert first, then US-deflate
> 
> 1007.5⁢11.2=11.11⁢ USD0.
> 
> ### Brazil-deflate first, then use the base-year exchange rate
> 
> 100×12.0×15=10.00⁢ USD0.
> 
> The first approach reports about 11% more in base-year USD. That difference is not an arithmetic error: it reflects the fact that the real’s depreciation was smaller than would have been implied by the Brazil–US inflation differential.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## A common incorrect hybrid
> 
> One sometimes sees:
> 
> 𝑋𝑡⁢𝑃𝐵⁢𝑅0𝑃𝐵⁢𝑅𝑡⁢1𝐸𝑡.
> 
> That is, deflate to **base-year reais**, but convert them using the **current-year nominal exchange rate**. This mixes units: a constant-price real is being converted using a rate that applies to current-price reais. It can be used only if one has explicitly constructed a corresponding **real exchange rate** conversion—not merely the nominal rate 𝐸𝑡.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Where PPP fits
> 
> For international comparisons of **real income, consumption, welfare, or domestic production**, neither route is usually ideal. Use a PPP conversion factor:
> 
> 𝑋Int⁢$𝑡=𝑋𝐵⁢𝑅⁢𝐿𝑡PPP𝐵⁢𝑅,𝑡,
> 
> where PPP𝐵⁢𝑅,𝑡 is in BRL per international dollar and corresponds to the relevant aggregate (GDP, actual individual consumption, etc.).
> 
> PPP conversion is intended to compare the purchasing power of currencies over a broadly comparable basket of goods and services. Market exchange rates instead value currency in international financial markets.
> 
> So, in short:
> 
> | Objective | Usually appropriate treatment |
> |---|---|
> | Value in internationally tradable/financial USD | Convert at market exchange rate, then use a US deflator if constant USD are needed |
> | Domestic Brazilian real quantity over time | Deflate using a Brazilian price index; retain BRL or convert using a fixed base-year conversion |
> | Cross-country welfare, consumption, or real output comparison | Use the relevant PPP conversion factor |
> | Comparing model monetary outputs across time and countries | Establish the model’s numeraire first; ideally report both market-USD and PPP-based results where their interpretations matter |
> 
> The key is that “real USD” is not a neutral universal unit. It means purchasing power in the **United States**; “real BRL” means purchasing power in **Brazil**; and PPP international dollars are constructed for a third purpose: cross-country real comparisons.
**user**:
> I'm trying to categorize different GDP datasets-- some use PPP, some MER, and I've just realized that some do national deflators first and some do the US conversion first. Now I'm wondering if the ordering if there's an additional "when is PPP" applied problem to think about. If I'm keeping track of this in a spreadsheet, with rows for different GDP datasets, what columns do I need to track and what possible entries could each have?
**assistant**:
> Yes. Once you distinguish **currency-conversion basis** from **time-price basis**, “PPP GDP” versus “MER GDP” is not enough. A dataset’s reported GDP reflects a conversion/deflation *pipeline*, and PPP can enter at different points.
> 
> The most useful spreadsheet structure is:
> 
> - one row per **dataset / variable / version** (not merely per source), with metadata describing its construction;
> - optionally a separate country–year table for the actual conversion-factor series used.
> 
> ## Core columns
> 
> | Column | Possible entries / examples | Why it matters |
> |---|---|---|
> | `dataset_id` | `WDI_NY.GDP.MKTP.KD`, `SSP2_GDP_PPP`, `IIASA_MESSAGE_GDP_MER` | Stable internal identifier |
> | `source` | World Bank WDI; OECD; IMF WEO; IIASA SSP database; national accounts | Provenance |
> | `version_or_vintage` | WDI 2026-07 download; SSP database release date; WEO Apr 2026 | PPP benchmarks, historical accounts, and revisions change |
> | `variable_name` | GDP; gross domestic product; consumption; GDP per capita | Avoid conflating GDP with related aggregates |
> | `national-accounts concept` | nominal GDP; real GDP / volume GDP; expenditure-side GDP; production-side GDP | “GDP” itself has accounting variants |
> | `geographic unit` | country; region; IAM region; global aggregate | Aggregation is especially consequential for PPP and MER |
> | `coverage` | historical; projected; historical + scenario projection | Historical and projected portions can use different methods |
> | `unit as supplied` | current LCU; constant 2015 LCU; current USD; constant 2015 USD; current Int$; constant 2021 Int$ | Record verbatim, not merely an interpretation |
> | `currency / numeraire` | local currency; USD; international dollar; model currency | The final reporting unit |
> | `conversion basis` | none / LCU; **MER**; **PPP**; hybrid; unclear | Main classification, but insufficient alone |
> | `price basis` | current-price / nominal; constant-price / real; chained-volume / reference-year prices; fixed-base volume | Separates inflation treatment from currency conversion |
> | `price reference year` | 2015; 2021; “current”; varies by country; not applicable | What “constant dollars” means |
> | `PPP reference / benchmark year` | ICP 2011; ICP 2017; ICP 2021; unspecified; annual/time-varying | Crucial for international-dollar series |
> | `PPP concept` | GDP PPP; actual individual consumption (AIC) PPP; household final consumption PPP; expenditure-specific PPP; unspecified | GDP PPP is not the right factor for household welfare/consumption |
> | `PPP series source` | ICP; World Bank WDI; Penn World Table; OECD-Eurostat; model-specific/IAMC legacy factors | PPP values depend materially on source/vintage |
> | `exchange-rate concept` | official market rate; period-average market rate; end-period rate; national-accounts implicit rate; model-specific; none | “USD” need not mean the same exchange-rate series |
> | `exchange-rate source` | IMF IFS; World Bank PA.NUS.FCRF; OECD; internal model series | Reproducibility |
> | `conversion-factor timing` | contemporaneous annual; fixed base-year; benchmark-year fixed; interpolated/extrapolated; projected/modelled | This is the key “when is PPP applied?” field |
> | `deflator / volume measure` | national GDP deflator; national chain-volume index; US GDP deflator; US CPI; PPP-based price adjustment; none | Identifies which economy’s inflation is removed |
> | `deflator source` | national accounts; WDI; BEA; IMF; model assumption | Deflator provenance |
> | `transformation order` | controlled vocabulary below | Captures the full pipeline |
> | `aggregation order` | convert then aggregate; aggregate in LCU impossible/mixed; regional model aggregate; unclear | Country PPP conversion and regional aggregation generally do not commute |
> | `historical vs projection methodology` | same; historical source + model projection; splice/rebase at 2015; unknown | Prevents assuming a scenario series is homogeneous |
> | `method confidence` | documented; inferred from documentation; inferred from values; unknown | Very useful in practice |
> | `documentation / citation` | URL, DOI, table note, code path | Audit trail |
> | `notes / caveats` | e.g. “MESSAGE MER conversion uses legacy IAMC PPP/MER ratios” | Free-text exceptions |
> 
> ---
> 
> ## The most important controlled-vocabulary column: `transformation_order`
> 
> I would make this an explicit field, rather than relying on a prose notes column. Suggested entries:
> 
> | Code | Transformation order | Typical interpretation |
> |---|---|---|
> | `LCU_CURRENT` | $Y_t^{LCU}$ | Nominal GDP in local currency; no deflation/conversion |
> | `LCU_REAL_NATIONAL` | $Y_t^{LCU} \times P_{0}^{nat}/P_t^{nat}$ | Real GDP in constant local currency using national deflator or volume index |
> | `MER_CURRENT` | $Y_t^{LCU}/e_t$ | Current USD at contemporaneous market exchange rates |
> | `MER_US_DEFLATED` | $\left(Y_t^{LCU}/e_t\right)\times P_0^{US}/P_t^{US}$ | Convert to current USD first, then express in constant US dollars |
> | `NATIONAL_DEFLATE_THEN_FIXED_MER` | $\left(Y_t^{LCU}\times P_0^{nat}/P_t^{nat}\right)/e_0$ | Deflate domestically, then use a base-year exchange rate |
> | `PPP_CURRENT_ANNUAL` | $Y_t^{LCU}/q_t$ | Current-price international dollars using the contemporaneous GDP PPP factor $q_t$ |
> | `NATIONAL_VOLUME_THEN_FIXED_PPP` | $\left(Y_t^{LCU}\times P_0^{nat}/P_t^{nat}\right)/q_0$ | Real local GDP converted using a fixed benchmark/base-year PPP |
> | `PPP_CURRENT_THEN_US_DEFLATED` | $\left(Y_t^{LCU}/q_t\right)\times P_0^{US}/P_t^{US}$ | Annual PPP conversion followed by US deflation; conceptually possible, but must be documented carefully |
> | `CHAIN_VOLUME_INTL_DOLLAR` | Source-defined chain-volume construction | Common in official databases; do **not** assume it is algebraically identical to a simple deflator-and-PPP formula |
> | `MODEL_NUMERAIRE` | model-specific | E.g., GDP generated directly in model units, sometimes labelled “USD” or “PPP” |
> | `UNKNOWN` | unknown | Honest and useful category |
> 
> Here:
> 
> - $e_t$ is the market exchange rate in LCU per USD;
> - $q_t$ is the GDP PPP conversion factor in LCU per international dollar;
> - $P_t^{nat}$ is a national GDP deflator or price index;
> - $P_t^{US}$ is a US deflator.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## The additional PPP timing problem
> 
> There are two conceptually distinct ways PPP may be used for a real, time-series GDP measure.
> 
> ### 1. Apply a **time-varying PPP** each year
> 
> \[
> Y_t^{Int\$,\,current} = \frac{Y_t^{LCU,current}}{q_t}.
> \]
> 
> This compares each year at that year’s relative price structure. But changes in $q_t$ reflect changes in relative prices, ICP updates, interpolation, and revisions—not solely changes in physical output or domestic purchasing power.
> 
> It is often appropriate for a **current-price cross-country comparison in a given year**, assuming annual PPPs are genuinely available and suitable.
> 
> ### 2. Measure real growth domestically, then apply a **fixed PPP** from a reference year
> 
> \[
> Y_t^{Int\$,\,0}
> =
> \frac{Y_t^{LCU,real\;in\;year\;0\;prices}}{q_0}.
> \]
> 
> This holds the international conversion benchmark fixed. It is generally the cleaner construction for a **time series of real output growth expressed in constant international dollars**.
> 
> For example, the intended interpretation is:
> 
> > “Brazil’s real GDP path, measured using Brazilian national-accounts volume growth, valued at the relative prices represented by the reference-year PPP benchmark.”
> 
> This avoids treating changes in the PPP conversion factor as GDP-volume growth.
> 
> ### Key warning
> 
> Do not automatically assume that “constant 2021 international dollars” means simply:
> 
> \[
> Y_t^{LCU,current}
> \times \frac{P_{2021}^{Brazil}}{P_t^{Brazil}}
> \div q_{2021}.
> \]
> 
> Official datasets may instead use chain-volume measures, benchmark-year PPPs, linked series, or source-specific volume extrapolations. Record the provider’s documented construction where possible.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## A compact classification that will probably serve your immediate purpose
> 
> For your main spreadsheet, I would ensure every row has these minimum fields:
> 
> ```text
> dataset_id
> source
> version_or_vintage
> country_or_region
> variable
> unit_as_published
> nominal_or_real
> price_reference_year
> final_numeraire
> conversion_basis                  # LCU / MER / PPP / model-specific
> conversion_factor_concept         # market FX / GDP PPP / AIC PPP / ...
> conversion_factor_source
> conversion_factor_timing          # annual / fixed-base / benchmark / projected
> deflator_or_volume_measure
> deflator_geography                # national / US / none
> transformation_order
> PPP_benchmark_year
> historical_projection_method
> aggregation_order
> documentation_link
> method_confidence
> notes
> ```
> 
> For datasets with ambiguous documentation, put `unknown` rather than filling in a plausible convention. “Constant USD” and “PPP GDP” labels are frequently underspecified.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Example rows
> 
> | Dataset | Unit as published | Conversion basis | Price basis | PPP/FX timing | Deflator geography | Transformation order |
> |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
> | National accounts Brazil GDP | current BRL | LCU | nominal | n/a | none | `LCU_CURRENT` |
> | National accounts Brazil real GDP | chained BRL, reference year 2021 | LCU | real/chained | n/a | Brazil | `LCU_REAL_NATIONAL` |
> | GDP in current USD | current USD | MER | nominal | annual FX | none | `MER_CURRENT` |
> | GDP in constant 2015 USD | constant 2015 USD | MER | real | annual FX then US deflator, *if that is its documented construction* | US | `MER_US_DEFLATED` |
> | GDP in constant 2021 Int$ | constant 2021 Int$ | GDP PPP | real | fixed/benchmark 2021 PPP,
