> I'm concerned with datasets that have many countries (so, Penn World Tables, World Development Indicators, CRU are the most common). How does that change this?
**assistant**:
> The core conceptual distinctions do **not** change, but for multi-country sources you should separate:
> 
> 1. **dataset-level methodology** — what the provider says it constructs;
> 2. **country-year conversion inputs** — which PPPs, exchange rates
**user**:
> Oh, to clarify, I meant that the rows will be these whole databases, not the individual country values within them.
**assistant**:
> If each row is a **whole database**, rather than a country or country-year, the key addition is a field for whether the database has a **single uniform GDP methodology** at all.
> 
> For example, **WDI is not one GDP series**: it publishes current LCU, constant LCU, current USD at MER, constant USD, current PPP international dollars, and constant PPP international dollars. A single row labelled “WDI” cannot truthfully have one value for “conversion basis” or “deflator order.”
> 
> So I would use either:
> 
> 1. **One row per database × GDP indicator/series × vintage** — analytically cleanest; or  
> 2. **One row per database**, with columns that describe its *portfolio of GDP representations* and flag that the construction depends on the selected indicator.
> 
> If you really want one row per database, use the second approach.
> 
> ## Suggested database-level spreadsheet columns
> 
> | Column | Example entries | Purpose |
> |---|---|---|
> | `database` | WDI; Penn World Table; CRU product name | Database-level identifier |
> | `edition_vintage` | WDI download 2026-08; PWT 11.0 | Essential: methods and PPP benchmarks change |
> | `GDP_series_available` | current LCU; constant LCU; current USD; constant USD; current Int$; constant Int$ | A semicolon-separated inventory of relevant fields |
> | `single_GDP_methodology` | yes; no—indicator-specific; partly | For WDI/PWT, usually `no—indicator-specific` |
> | `primary_series_used` | `NY.GDP.MKTP.KD`; `rgdpo`; `rgdpna` | Even if the row is the whole database, this says what *you* are actually using |
> | `published_unit` | constant 2015 USD; million 2021 Int$; index; national currency | Record the literal source unit |
> | `nominal_or_real` | nominal/current-price; real/constant-price; chained volume; both | |
> | `real-growth construction` | national-accounts constant-price; national chain-volume; PWT chained PPP; modelled/estimated; unclear | More informative than merely “deflator” |
> | `conversion_basis` | LCU only; MER; PPP; both MER and PPP; model-specific | Database-level availability or selected-series basis |
> | `MER_treatment` | contemporaneous annual market FX; fixed base-year FX; official exchange-rate series; multiple | |
> | `PPP_treatment` | none; annual PPP; fixed reference-year PPP; chained PPP; multiple | The important “when is PPP applied?” category |
> | `PPP_concept` | GDP PPP; expenditure-side PPP; output-side PPP; AIC PPP; multiple | PWT in particular has distinct expenditure- and output-side concepts |
> | `PPP_benchmark_and_source` | ICP 2021 / World Bank; PWT internal benchmark-and-extrapolation system; multiple | |
> | `price_reference_year` | 2015; 2021; country-specific; series-specific; current prices | |
> | `deflation_or_conversion_order` | national-volume-first then fixed MER; national-volume-first then fixed PPP; current LCU → annual FX → US deflator; source-defined chained PPP; varies by series | Your central comparison field |
> | `country_method_heterogeneity` | low; moderate; high; unknown | Whether countries differ in source quality, national accounting practices, extrapolation, etc. |
> | `missing-PPP / nonbenchmark-country treatment` | interpolation/extrapolation; regression/imputation; regional proxy; not documented | Important for broad-coverage databases |
