> | `regional/global aggregation rule` | sum after country conversion; source-provided aggregate; PPP-weighted; not applicable | Relevant if you use database regional totals |
> | `cross-country-level comparability` | strong; reasonable with caveats; limited; not intended | A useful bottom-line assessment |
> | `cross-time growth comparability` | strong; reasonable with caveats; limited; not intended | Often differs from level comparability |
> | `documentation` | URL / DOI / metadata note | |
> | `classification_confidence` | documented; partly inferred; unclear | Prevents false precision |
> | `notes` | Free text | E.g., “Different PWT real-GDP concepts should not be interchanged.” |
> 
> ## The critical column: `deflation_or_conversion_order`
> 
> For a whole-database row, I would use a controlled vocabulary like this:
> 
> | Entry | Meaning |
> |---|---|
> | `Multiple—indicator-specific` | The database offers several series with different pipelines. This is the right default for WDI. |
> | `National volume first → fixed-base MER` | Real GDP is constructed in national prices, then converted at the exchange rate of a reference year. |
> | `National volume first → fixed-base PPP` | Real GDP is constructed in national prices, then expressed in international dollars using a fixed-year PPP. |
> | `Current LCU → annual MER → US deflator` | Convert at each year’s market exchange rate, then deflate in US prices. |
> | `Current LCU → annual PPP` | Current-price PPP-valued GDP for each year. |
> | `Chained PPP / source-defined international-volume measure` | A PPP-based real-growth measure whose construction is not reducible to one fixed PPP factor. |
> | `Model-specific or undocumented` | Common in scenario/model databases. |
> 
> For your purposes, distinguish **fixed-year PPP** from **annual/chained PPP**. They have materially different interpretations:
> 
> - **Fixed-year PPP after national real-GDP construction**: primarily a *real growth series* expressed in a common international-dollar unit.
> - **Annual PPP conversion**: primarily a *cross-sectional value comparison* for each year; changes over time contain both domestic volume/price changes and changes in relative international prices/PPPs.
> - **Chained PPP**: intended to improve temporal comparisons, but may sacrifice simple additivity or a single transparent “PPP factor.”
> 
> ## How this applies to the major databases
> 
> At a high level:
> 
> | Database | Appropriate database-level label |
> |---|---|
> | **World Development Indicators** | `Multiple—indicator-specific`; mostly republishes national-account series and conversion-factor-derived indicators. Its current/constant USD and current/constant PPP indicators must be classified separately. |
> | **Penn World Table** | `Multiple—concept-specific`; designed for internationally comparable real-income/product measures. In particular, distinguish `rgdpo` (output-side real GDP), `rgdpe` (expenditure-side real GDP), and `rgdpna` (real GDP at constant national prices). Do not treat all as generic “PPP GDP.” |
> | **CRU** | Depends on the exact product. “CRU” is not, by itself, a single standardized GDP database or GDP-conversion methodology. Record the particular CRU socioeconomic/exposure product, its GDP source, and whether CRU transformed it or simply adopted it. |
> 
> ## A compact practical template
> 
> If you want a manageable high-level comparison sheet, I would use:
> 
> ```text
> database
> edition_vintage
> primary_series_used
> GDP_series_available
> published_unit
> nominal_or_real
> conversion_basis
> PPP_treatment
> PPP_concept
> price_reference_year
> deflation_or_conversion_order
> country_method_heterogeneity
> missing_PPP_treatment
> regional_aggregation_rule
> cross_country_level_comparability
> cross_time_growth_comparability
> documentation
> classification_confidence
> notes
> ```
> 
> The most important practical rule is:
> 
> > A database name is not a monetary methodology.
> 
> For **WDI**, you should normally create separate rows for the exact indicators you use. For **PWT**, create separate rows for the exact real-GDP concept (`rgdpo`, `rgdpe`, `rgdpna`, etc.). You can still retain a higher-level “database” column and group them in the spreadsheet.
