> The important substantive interpretation is:
> 
> > Growth in `RGDPCH` is not necessarily the same as growth in a country’s national constant-price GDP per capita, rescaled by one fixed PPP conversion factor.
> 
> Because it is a **chain series**, the relative-price/PPP comparison framework can affect its growth path. Thus it can be very useful for internationally comparative real-income work, but it is not conceptually identical to “national GDP-volume growth in PPP units.”
> 
> For an analysis specifically about **national economic growth**, you might prefer a national-prices real GDP-per-capita series (in PWT, where available, the relevant national-accounts-style real GDP concept) and then use PPP only for a reference-year level comparison. For internationally comparable **GDPpc levels and growth**, `RGDPCH` may be the more defensible primary series—provided you label it accurately and test robustness against the national-prices alternative.
