> This lets you run a clean empirical comparison: estimate the same model repeatedly with one series from each family, then compare coefficient signs, magnitudes, uncertainty intervals, fit, or out-of-sample performance.
> 
> For example, your initial comparison set for GDPpc growth might be:
> 
> 1. **National-real baseline** — GDPpc growth from national constant-price GDP and a common population series.
> 2. **Fixed-PPP version** — if available; expected to be nearly identical in growth to (1).
> 3. **PWT chained-PPP version** — e.g. `RGDPCH` growth.
> 4. **MER/USD version** — useful as a deliberately exchange-rate-sensitive contrast.
> 5. **Annual-PPP version** — useful as a relative-price/PPP-sensitive contrast, if a defensible series exists.
> 
> The substantive expectation is:
> 
> - `national_real`, `fixed_PPP_real`, and `fixed_MER_real` should often have essentially the **same within-country growth rates** if derived from the same real-GDP source and population denominator.
> - `chained_PPP_real` may differ meaningfully, especially historically or in lower-data-quality countries.
> - `annual_MER`, `US_deflated_MER`, and `annual_PPP` can differ substantially, because currency or PPP movements enter the growth measure.
> 
> So, if you want the truly lean version, the only methodological columns needed are:
> 
> ```text
> collection_id
> source_version
> series
> effective_measure_type
> PPP_treatment
> price_basis
> reference_year
> coverage
> notes
> ```
> 
> Everything else is optional.
